WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.290 --> 00:00:06.660
Spider-Man: Homecoming stars the third live-action
Spider-Man since 2002, and that barely even

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:09.800
begins to scratch the surface of how many
Spider-Men have appeared onscreen over the

00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:10.300
years.

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:11.520
"(speaking Japanese)"

00:00:16.060 --> 00:00:17.400
So how do they stack up?

00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:22.600
Here's a look at the major versions of Spider-Man
from TV and movies—and the actors who played

00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:24.600
them—ranked from worst to best.

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:27.560
'70s Live Action Spider-Man

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:33.300
It isn't actor Nicholas Hammond's fault that
nobody remembers the short-lived Amazing Spider-Man

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:35.230
TV show from the late 1970s.

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:40.820
He just didn't have much to work with: there
were no supervillains, no supporting cast

00:00:40.820 --> 00:00:45.890
from the comics except J. Jonah Jameson and
a single appearance by Aunt May, and Hammond

00:00:45.890 --> 00:00:50.400
never looked, talked, or moved like anything
other than a random guy you'd run into at

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:50.900
ComicCon.

00:00:57.540 --> 00:00:58.740
Spider-Man Unlimited

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:04.240
Spider-Man Unlimited was basically an effort
to extend the '90s animated series past its

00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:07.280
sell-by date by sending Spider-Man to an alternate
Earth.

00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:12.540
Voice actor Rino Romano just did his best
impression of Christopher Daniel Barnes, who

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:13.520
preceded him in the role.

00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:17.100
"Great, if only carting around is my worst
nightmare.

00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:18.160
It's leaking!"

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:24.630
The costume, derived from the Spider-Man 2099
comics, is memorable, but it probably would

00:01:24.630 --> 00:01:28.880
have worked even better if they'd just made
a Spider-Man 2099 cartoon.

00:01:29.380 --> 00:01:31.280
'60s Animated Spider-Man

00:01:31.580 --> 00:01:36.140
Considering Spider-Man had only existed for
four years when this show started, relatively

00:01:36.140 --> 00:01:39.500
few viewers had a clear idea of just what
Spider-Man should be like.

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:46.810
Paul Soles as Spider-Man sounds the way 1960s
cartoon viewers expected a superhero to sound—which

00:01:46.810 --> 00:01:48.960
means he sounds exactly like Superman.

00:01:49.420 --> 00:01:53.300
"I once swore to never turn my back on anyone,
and I'm not starting now."

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:58.060
This Spider-Man is best remembered today as
the star of many animated GIFs and memes.

00:01:58.060 --> 00:01:59.920
And of course for its awesome theme song.

00:02:04.640 --> 00:02:08.560
"Spider-Man, Spider-Man, does whatever a spider
can."

00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:11.280
1981 Animated Spider-Man

00:02:11.720 --> 00:02:15.820
The 1981 Spider-Man animated series wasn't
bad by any means.

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:20.820
The animation was solid for the time, and
it brought in a lot of Spider-villains from

00:02:20.820 --> 00:02:23.120
the comics who'd never gotten TV time before.

00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:27.920
The weirdest thing about this show is that
it was immediately overshadowed by another

00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:33.200
Spider-Man cartoon, Spider-Man and His Amazing
Friends, which premiered the same day.

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:38.599
The two series also used the same character
designs, which kind of made this series feel

00:02:38.599 --> 00:02:43.719
like a run of Spider-Man and His Amazing Friends
in which he has no friends and talks a little

00:02:43.720 --> 00:02:46.139
funny, thanks to the vocal talents of Ted
Schwartz.

00:02:46.860 --> 00:02:50.520
"Oh no, I'm gonna miss my lunch date with
Betty."

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:52.300
The Electric Company

00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:57.159
Spider-Man's first live-action appearance,
interestingly enough, was on The Electric

00:02:57.160 --> 00:03:00.980
Company, a 1970s educational series that ran
on PBS.

00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:06.260
This Spider-Man, played by Danny Seagren,
didn't talk, but his simple adventures were

00:03:06.260 --> 00:03:09.820
the perfect vehicle for teaching kids basic
language and math skills.

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:11.600
But the highlight of the show?

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:15.660
The narration, which was provided by a pre-fame
Morgan Freeman.

00:03:16.220 --> 00:03:23.139
"And he's found it – a rubber glove sandwich."

00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:26.100
Spider-Man: The New Animated Series

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:31.480
Two things are distracting about 2003's Spider-Man:
The New Animated Series.

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:34.250
The first is the combo 2D and 3D animation.

00:03:34.250 --> 00:03:40.200
The second is that every time Spider-Man talks,
you can't help but hear Neil Patrick Harris.

00:03:40.940 --> 00:03:43.260
"This is so not spandex!"

00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:48.560
Not that Harris is a bad choice for Spider-Man,
but come on, isn't he more suited for villainy?

00:03:49.020 --> 00:03:50.480
Japanese Spider-Man

00:03:50.940 --> 00:03:57.079
The 1978 Japanese Spider Man live action TV
series has a cult following in the U.S., even

00:03:57.080 --> 00:03:59.580
though it's never been officially released
there.

00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:05.900
In this version, Spider-Man is a motorcycle
driver who gets his spider-powers from a super-bracelet

00:04:05.900 --> 00:04:07.380
from the planet Spider.

00:04:07.790 --> 00:04:12.680
The show's a lot of fun, and the actors do
a great job, but it just feels wrong to rank

00:04:12.700 --> 00:04:16.460
them any higher when they're not playing the
same Spider-Man as everybody else.

00:04:23.240 --> 00:04:23.740
"(speaking Japanese)"

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:28.000
2000s Spider-Man Movies

00:04:28.420 --> 00:04:33.400
In the early days of 21st-century superhero
movies, Tobey Maguire got the job done.

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:38.700
He got in great physical shape for Spider-Man,
but he still had the softness to play nerdy

00:04:38.700 --> 00:04:39.300
Peter Parker.

00:04:40.120 --> 00:04:41.460
"Punch me, I bleed."

00:04:42.280 --> 00:04:47.390
While he never quite seemed like a kid in
the way an origin-story Spider-Man should,

00:04:47.390 --> 00:04:52.660
he was still young enough be believable as
a teenager, making him a solid version of

00:04:52.660 --> 00:04:53.660
the web-slinger.

00:04:53.900 --> 00:04:55.160
Ultimate Spider-Man

00:04:55.680 --> 00:05:00.640
Disney's first animated Spider-Man series
after it purchased Marvel, some of the production

00:05:00.650 --> 00:05:05.610
decisions for Ultimate Spider-Man didn't sit
well with longtime fans – especially the

00:05:05.610 --> 00:05:09.780
decision to hire Drake Bell of Drake and Josh
to voice the title character.

00:05:09.780 --> 00:05:12.110
"You don't know how that feels."

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:13.240
"I do, actually.

00:05:15.140 --> 00:05:17.020
Someone important was taken from me, too."

00:05:17.220 --> 00:05:21.860
But if you look past the bad buzz, Bell is
actually a pretty great animated Spider-Man,

00:05:21.860 --> 00:05:26.840
and he also comes through in the darker moments
that require a more emotional Peter Parker.

00:05:27.640 --> 00:05:29.080
Amazing Spider-Man

00:05:29.500 --> 00:05:33.750
The biggest problem with Andrew Garfield as
Peter Parker is that he was already too old

00:05:33.750 --> 00:05:36.380
for the part when he starred in The Amazing
Spider-Man.

00:05:37.020 --> 00:05:42.530
He never seems like an awkward teen, and yet
the rebooted franchise offered another origin

00:05:42.530 --> 00:05:44.000
story set in high school.

00:05:44.360 --> 00:05:49.200
Still, his slim build looked good in the suit,
and his comic timing made him a natural fit

00:05:49.200 --> 00:05:52.080
for the rhythms of the character's constant
wisecracks.

00:05:54.100 --> 00:05:54.680
"Knock it off man!"

00:05:54.920 --> 00:05:55.480
"no NO!"

00:05:56.820 --> 00:05:58.700
'90s Animated Spider-Man

00:05:59.180 --> 00:06:04.360
For a lot of millennials, Christopher Daniel
Barnes was the definitive voice of Spider-Man

00:06:04.360 --> 00:06:05.220
throughout their childhood.

00:06:05.760 --> 00:06:10.300
While this Spider-Man has aged a little awkwardly,
it was the first TV version of the character

00:06:10.301 --> 00:06:15.560
to really draw on the comics' storylines in
a nuanced way, introducing concepts like the

00:06:15.560 --> 00:06:19.240
Venom suit, the Secret Wars crossover, and
even Daredevil.

00:06:19.780 --> 00:06:23.360
This series skipped the origin and began with
Peter Parker already in college.

00:06:23.720 --> 00:06:28.600
Within that context, Barnes is perfect in
the part, balancing the jokes and the emotional

00:06:28.600 --> 00:06:30.840
baggage that are both so central to Spider-Man.

00:06:31.660 --> 00:06:33.000
"Spider sense - danger!"

00:06:34.900 --> 00:06:36.820
Spider-Man and His Amazing Friends

00:06:37.360 --> 00:06:42.040
For those a little older, Dan Gilvezan was
the Saturday morning Spider-Man of choice.

00:06:42.040 --> 00:06:47.660
He voiced the central hero on Spider-Man and
His Amazing Friends from 1981 until 1986.

00:06:48.120 --> 00:06:52.040
His Spider-Man had the lightness that earlier
versions of the character had been missing,

00:06:52.040 --> 00:06:55.780
while still seeming more grounded than the
goofier Iceman.

00:06:55.780 --> 00:06:56.760
"Oh, no."

00:06:59.020 --> 00:06:59.880
"Oh, yes!"

00:07:00.380 --> 00:07:05.440
This cartoon seems pretty silly by today's
standards, but this was the first time Spider-Man

00:07:05.440 --> 00:07:08.080
really seemed like Spider-Man, and that's
worth something.

00:07:08.620 --> 00:07:10.200
The Spectacular Spider-Man

00:07:10.800 --> 00:07:15.600
Spectacular Spider-Man was basically the perfect
Spider-Man cartoon, and one that ended too

00:07:15.640 --> 00:07:16.140
soon.

00:07:16.760 --> 00:07:22.860
It ran for two seasons in 2008 and 2009, and
was being considered for a third when Disney

00:07:22.870 --> 00:07:27.950
bought Marvel and reacquired the television
rights to Spider-Man, leaving this Sony-produced

00:07:27.950 --> 00:07:29.300
series unable to continue.

00:07:29.860 --> 00:07:34.980
Voice actor Josh Keaton played the youthful
Spider-Man with the earnestness of a kid trying

00:07:34.980 --> 00:07:36.820
to do his best, and it really worked.

00:07:37.040 --> 00:07:39.040
It's just a shame there couldn't be more of
it.

00:07:39.240 --> 00:07:40.240
"Uh, yoo-hoo!

00:07:40.620 --> 00:07:43.800
Excuse me, can I come in this way or do I
have to break my own window?"

00:07:44.140 --> 00:07:45.080
"Spider-Man."

00:07:45.760 --> 00:07:47.380
MCU Spider-Man

00:07:47.960 --> 00:07:52.860
As the third live-action Spidey in under a
decade, Tom Holland may finally be the perfect

00:07:52.860 --> 00:07:53.360
choice.

00:07:53.870 --> 00:07:58.300
He comes across like a young teenager with
a surprising amount of power, and his youthfulness

00:07:58.310 --> 00:08:02.930
stands out perfectly in contrast with all
the adult heroes running around in the MCU.

00:08:02.930 --> 00:08:03.920
"Are you an Avenger?"

00:08:05.460 --> 00:08:06.380
"Yeah, basically."

00:08:06.730 --> 00:08:11.720
Spider-Man: Homecoming presents him as a nerdy
high school student who might be in way over

00:08:11.730 --> 00:08:16.540
his head—and that's exactly what Spider-Man
should be, especially within the context of

00:08:16.540 --> 00:08:18.660
a larger Avengers-centric setting.

00:08:19.200 --> 00:08:20.200
Thanks for watching!

00:08:20.360 --> 00:08:23.040
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:08:23.040 --> 00:08:25.020
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

